#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/id #uuid "833de87d-9983-5f8d-94aa-3132606fc15d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3775" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\cos{2x} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$ If the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ and $x_4$ are written in ascending order,  what is the value of $x_2\\cdot x_3?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{8n\\pi }{8} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{8}+\\frac{8n\\pi }{8} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(8n+1)\\pi }{8} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(8n+7)\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}$ and $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{8}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}$ and $x=\\dfrac{15\\pi}{8}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1, x_2, x_3$ and $x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{7\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{9\\pi}{8}, \\, \\dfrac{15\\pi}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nx_2\\cdot x_3 =  \\dfrac{7\\pi}{8} \\cdot  \\dfrac{9\\pi}{8} = \\dfrac{63\\pi^2}{64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092173 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1286cc9b-2080-5541-88e1-d4d2741ea1e5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by Multiple Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "d8397378-838b-5fb1-b3e7-b5c341a17e38" :question/math-academy-id "e-3784" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\left(2x+60^\\circ\\right) - 3 = 0$ for $0^\\circ\\leq x <           360^\\circ.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘})-3 & =0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{60}^{∘}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{60}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{60}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={150}^{∘}-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={210}^{∘}-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=45^\\circ$ and $x=75^\\circ.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=225^\\circ$  and $x=255^\\circ.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x \\lt 360^\\circ.$ So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 45^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 75^\\circ,\\quad x_3 =  225^\\circ,\\quad x_4= 255^\\circ .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092174 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e880672-e776-5701-8168-730f7012f5b0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing a Transformed Cosine Function Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "c672314a-e7f7-5f64-9f59-b49698b76b37" :question/math-academy-id "e-3791" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\cos\\left(2x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ for $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{(24n-11)\\pi }{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(24n+5)\\pi }{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{59\\pi}{24}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{43\\pi}{24}.$ We reject the solution $-\\dfrac{59\\pi}{24}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{35\\pi}{24}$ and $x=-\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\dfrac{11\\pi}{24}$ and $x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{24}.$ We reject the solution $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{24}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-2\\pi <          x \\leq 0.$   So, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{43\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_2=-\\dfrac{35\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_3 = -\\dfrac{19\\pi}{24}, \\quad x_4 = -\\dfrac{11\\pi}{24}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542638} {:answer/id #uuid "32606e7b-b40b-50e3-a1d1-5d1910806be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "370" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542774} {:answer/id #uuid "78fc0312-8634-566a-bd85-e6da768f8465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542961} {:answer/id #uuid "90057f88-a3e0-5ac7-8640-ecf0b41f4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "430" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543016} {:answer/id #uuid "be134ae9-dcf1-5a55-b9fd-d851e56132fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "390" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "011df462-573f-5d1e-a6c9-530e8e4108d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542638} :answer-field/id #uuid "e903e461-23e0-5fcd-b90c-a7d9090e44fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "597abfab-9fa0-5884-9650-9d3a1ca7b418" :question/math-academy-id "q-121513" :question/problem "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are written in ascending order,  what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={480}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{{240}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{480}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {720}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={80}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={160}^{∘}+n\\cdot {240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={80}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={320}^{∘}$ and $x={400}^{∘}.$ We reject the solution ${400}^{∘}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={80}^{∘},\\,{160}^{∘},\\,{320}^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}={80}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={320}^{∘},$ the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\text{ }+{x}_{3}$ is $80+320=400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d2769f-5f35-5619-a06a-6da4d0cd9d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542734} {:answer/id #uuid "3adfe199-8aa8-54b5-af09-00ff18169df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542801} {:answer/id #uuid "66f3e329-d6ad-5cda-b6ea-849f556393e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8{\\pi }^{2}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542916} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543052} {:answer/id #uuid "b72530e6-e6de-50bd-a6e2-1633d11cef2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4b999-29de-5078-b62a-6736401df8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543052} :answer-field/id #uuid "babdd4ab-5b3d-5b5f-8892-4e5632d1283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c08c7d82-b9b5-59ff-a4f6-2f1eac5d7e57" :question/math-academy-id "q-121537" :question/problem "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{5}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{5x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 5x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{15}+\\frac{4n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{15} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+7)\\pi }{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{15}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{15}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{15}$  and $x=\\frac{31\\pi }{15}.$ We reject the solution $\\frac{31\\pi }{15}$ since it lies outside the domain of interest.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{17\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{19\\pi }{15},\\,\\frac{29\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{15}=\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{45}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6d31a25-823d-59b2-897c-61a75b747c01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing the Cosine Function Transformed by One Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed8ee43d-321d-5eba-941d-0c34f9da8b72" 